3. Non-functional requirements
                        This is where the interview becomes interesting.
                        
                        YouTube is primarily a massively read-heavy system.
                        
                        We want:                        
                                High availability                        
                                                Users should still be able to watch videos even if some infrastructure fails.
                        
                                Low playback latency                        
                                                When a user clicks a video, playback should begin quickly.
                                                This is often called: Time to First Frame (TTFF)

                                High scalability
                                                We need to support enormous numbers of:                                                
                                                                uploads
                                                                video views
                                                                concurrent streams
                                                                searches

                                Durability
                                        Video data is extremely valuable.                                        
                                        Losing uploaded videos is unacceptable.
                                        
                                Global availability                                        
                                        Users are distributed geographically.
                                        A user in India shouldn't need to retrieve every video byte from a server in the US.
================================================================================================================================================================
4. Back-of-the-envelope estimation
                At L5, don't skip this.                
                Let's make reasonable assumptions.
                
                Suppose:                        
                        2 billion monthly active users
                        500 million daily active users
                        100 million videos uploaded/day
                        5 billion video views/day
                
                We don't need exact real-world numbers. We're demonstrating architectural reasoning.

                Video views
                                5 billion / 86,400
                                ≈ 57,870 views/sec
                
                Average playback requests are therefore tens of thousands per second.                
                But that's only the average.
                
                Peak traffic could easily be:    5x – 10x average

                So we should design for hundreds of thousands of playback-related requests/sec.




